INSERT INTO sessions VALUES('f785b9b7-636','/tmp','openai:ornith-1.5-35b','custom','ou are continuing the `agpack` project (portable, verifiable','builder',319,NULL,'[]',0,0,NULL,NULL,'sandbox-capability-engine',0,'2026-08-27 16:19:52','{}','{"boundary_index": 302, "summary_text": "[dcode-proxy: the model spent its entire token budget reasoning and never emitted an answer. Its reasoning is shown below. Raise max_tokens to get a real answer.]\n\nLet me carefully analyze this compaction task. I need to produce a structured summary of the conversation, folding in the previous compaction summary''s still-relevant content, and the conversation-since window.\n\nLet me trace through what''s happened:\n\nThe overarching project is \"agpack\" - a WASM tool sandbox (Step 2 of the project). The driver interface design was settled: host hands driver (module_bytes, imports, budget, audit), driver returns RunResult-shaped object.\n\nThe assistant has been building probe scripts in /tmp to validate the wasmtime 48.0.0 API and WASM module encoding.\n\nLet me extract the key technical findings from this turn:\n\n1. **Section ordering issue**: The type section count `vuint(3)` was leaking into section-header slot because the section wasn''t wrapped with section-id + size.\n\n2. **Memory section issue**: The memory section body must be `[count=01][flag=00][min=01]` = size 3.\n\n3. **Body size computation**: The key realization - hand-encoding section sizes fails because they don''t match actual bodies. Need to `section(id, body)` helper that computes size from body length.\n\n4. **Import count byte**: Import section needs leading `vuint(number_of_imports)` count, not the body length.\n\n5. **The walker trap**: The hand-walker blindly follows declared sizes, so it can''t catch a wrongly-computed size. wasmtime''s actual error messages are authoritative.\n\n6. **Memory import vs Memory section**: Every memory-section encoding fails while type+import (func imports only) parses fine. The realization: import memory from the host (no Memory section). The sandbox design imports `env/memory` anyway, and having both an import and a Memory section is a duplicate.\n\nWait, but then they hit \"exported function index out of bounds\" - the code section encoding issue.\n\n7. **Code body encoding**: The function body is just `locals` + `code bytes`, with no extra vector wrapper. So `00 0b` (zero locals + end) is correct, but they had a spurious `01`.\n\n8. **Function section (id 12)**: The code section requires a preceding Function section mapping each code index to its type index \u2014 \"function section is absent\".\n\n9. **Section order**: WASM canonical section order requires Code (10) before Function (12).\n\n10. **Function type index embedded in code body**: The current spec embedded the type index at the start of each function body in the Code section (or a separate Function section). wasmtime said \"function section is absent but code section has non-zero count\".\n\n11. **\"unknown function 2\"**: wasmtime counts fewer than 3 functions. Uncertain whether wasmtime wants typeidx embedded in code body or separate Function section.\n\nLet me look more carefully at the last few steps. The conversation got progressively more tangled. At the very end:\n\n- \"unknown function 2\" issue\n- They tried a clean matrix\n- Then switched to writing probe via `cat > /tmp/probe.py` heredoc\n- The realization: In the Code section, each function body is encoded as `[body_size][body]`, and each body starts with `typeidx + locals + code`. They''ve been writing `vuint(1)+func_body` but omitting the per-function body size prefix. Build exactly: body = `typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b)` = 3 bytes, wrapped as `count(01) body_size(03) body`.\n\nWait, `typeidx(02)` - that means type index 2? Hmm, let me re-read. \"body = typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b) = 3 bytes, wrapped as count(01) body_size(03) body\".\n\nHmm, so they''re saying each function body starts with typeidx. Actually