he assistant''s current plan is to build probe.py with this encoding. The `cat > /tmp/probe.py` command was shown but I need to check if it was actually run. Looking at the last entries:\n\n\"[assistant \u2192 run_shell] {\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/probe.py << ''EOF''...type_sec += b\\\"\\\\x60\\\"+vuint(0)+vuint(1)+b\\\"\\\\x7f\\\" # t0 ()->i3\u2026\"\n\"[assistant \u2192 run_shell] {\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/probe.py << ''EOF''...func_body=...}\"\n\"[assistant \u2192 run_shell] {\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/probe.py << ''EOF''...In the Code section, each function body is encoded as [body_size][body]...wrapped as count(01) body_size(03) body.\"\n\nSo there are multiple `cat > /tmp/probe.py` attempts. The last one is the assistant narrating the fix but the command content is cut off. So the actual execution state is unclear - the last tool call may or may not have been run. It seems the assistant was describing the final encoding approach.\n\nGiven the truncation, I''ll note that the final probe.py via heredoc was being written with the \"body = typeidx + locals + code\" encoding, and its execution result is not shown yet.\n\nNow let me organize the wasmtime API findings:\n\nFrom previous compaction (still relevant) + this turn:\n- `ValType.i32()`, `ValType.i64()` \u2014 not `wasmtime.Kind.i32`\n- `FuncType([...vals], [...vals])` \u2014 note: results must be a LIST `[ValType.i32()]`, not a single `ValType.i32()`\n- `Config()`: `cfg.wasm_multi_value = True`, `cfg.consume_fuel = True` (property setter, NOT `cfg.consume_fuel(True)`)\n- `Engine(cfg)`, `Store(engine)` or `Store(cfg)`\n- `Linker(engine)`\n- `Memory(store, MemoryType(wasmtime.Limits(min, max)))`\n- `MemoryType(limits, is_64=False, shared=False, page_size_log2=16)`\n- `Limits(min, max)` \u2014 min, max are u32s\n- `linker.define(store, module, name, item)` \u2014 NOT `define_w_func`\n- `Module(engine, module_bytes)` \u2014 note `Module(engine, bytes)`, not `Module(store, bytes)`\n- `linker.instantiate(module)` \u2014 `lk.instantiate(Module(eng, full))`\n- `instance.exports` exists\n- `store.set_fuel(n)`, `store.get_fuel()`\n- `wasmtime.__version__` throws AttributeError\n\nWASM binary encoding:\n- Magic: `\\x00\\x61\\x73\\x6d`, version `\\x01\\x00\\x00\\x00`\n- Section IDs: type=0x01, import=0x02, export=0x07, code=0x0a (10), function=0x0c (12), memory=0x03\n- Each section: `section_id` (1 byte), then `vuint(len)`, then content\n- `vuint(n)`: LEB128 varuint\n- `name(s)`: `vuint(len(s)) + s.encode()`\n- `section(id, body)` helper: `bytes([id]) + vuint(len(body)) + body` \u2014 CRUCIAL to compute size from body\n\nFunction types:\n- `(i32,i32,i32)->i32` = `60 03 7f 7f 7f 01 7f`\n- `()->i32` = `60 00 01 7f`\n- `()->()` = `60 00 00 00`\n\nImport entry: `name(module)` + `name(name)` + kind_byte + kind_data\n- func import (kind 0x00): `vuint(type_index)`\n- memory import (kind 0x02): flag + min (min-only, unbounded: flag 0x00)\n- import section starts with `vuint(number_of_imports)` (count), NOT body length\n\nCode section:\n- count of functions + for each: body_size + body\n- body = typeidx + locals(00) + code... (this is what the assistant concluded, but see note below about confusion)\n\nWait, I need to be careful. Let me reconsider the WASM encoding. The assistant''s confusion:\n\nIn one step, they said the code body is `locals + code bytes` with no vector wrapper, and `00 0b` is correct (zero locals + end).\n\nIn another step, they said wasmtime said \"function section is absent but code section has non-zero count\" \u2014 which means wasmtime WANTS the Function section (id 12).\n\nIn the final step, they concluded: \"body = typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b) = 3 bytes, wrapped as count(01) body_size(03) body.\"\n\nThere''s genuine confusion in the transcript about whether:\n(a) Use a separate Function section (id 12) to map type indices, OR\n(b) Embed typeidx in the code body\n\nThe assistant flip-flopped. The message \"function section is absent but code section has non-zero