INSERT INTO sessions VALUES('1472646f-019','/home/iconbaypark2900/openworker-tasks/1472646f-019','openai:ornith-1.5-35b','auto','Let''s go over what was built from the last builder session f','fastchat',310,NULL,'[{"path": "/home/iconbaypark2900/dataScience/agpack", "writable": false, "label": "agpack"}, {"path": "/home/iconbaypark2900/OpenWorker", "writable": false, "label": "OpenWorker"}]',0,0,NULL,NULL,'Agpack Project Build Review',0,'2026-08-29 01:24:50','{}','{"boundary_index": 285, "summary_text": "[dcode-proxy: the model spent its entire token budget reasoning and never emitted an answer. Its reasoning is shown below. Raise max_tokens to get a real answer.]\n\nLet me carefully analyze this conversation to build a structured summary. The conversation has a previous compaction summary folded in, then new conversation turns since.\n\nLet me trace through what happened:\n\n1. The conversation started with the assistant reading three remaining sandbox test files to complete an inventory of the agpack test suite:\n   - test_sandbox_host.py\n   - test_sandbox_limits.py\n   - test_sandbox_imports.py\n\n2. The assistant noted it read 5 of 9 test files, but the listing was truncated, and there were 13 \"unknown\" files.\n\n3. The assistant did a brain_note recording that the stale reproduce file `.scratch/reproduce_full.txt` is gone, the full suite is 203 tests (82 trust + sandbox 34 + artifact bundle).\n\n4. The assistant did todo_write with 4 todos.\n\n5. The assistant then read test_artifact_bundle.py and test_sandbox_capabilities.py.\n\n6. The assistant did list_files on the tests directory to get the complete list.\n\n7. The assistant read the 3 trust test files (test_trust_audit.py, test_trust_delegation.py, test_trust_signing.py).\n\n8. The assistant realized only 8 test files total exist (not 9), and the \"13 unknown files\" was a misread.\n\n9. The assistant did grep \"^def test_\" but got \"path escapes the workspace\" error (agpack is outside the openworker-tasks workspace).\n\n10. The assistant wrote verify_agpack_tests.md in the workspace with the full report.\n\n11. The assistant counted and reconciled: 29+29+24 + 30+25+28+25 + 13 = 203. The stale \"10 failed / 72 passed\" was wrong.\n\n12. The user asked \"What is next to do?\"\n\n13. The assistant read validator.py, grepped for yaml/pyyaml/pydantic in .toml files, listed files.\n\n14. The assistant confirmed the production change (import pydantic + import yaml) was already applied in validator.py.\n\n15. The assistant checked pyproject.toml and _verify_all.py.\n\n16. The assistant identified the real next step: declare pydantic and PyYAML as runtime deps in pyproject.toml.\n\n17. The user responded \"Done. Both parts verified.\" \u2014 the manifest was fixed, and a clean pytest run showed 203 passed.\n\n18. The assistant did brain_note recording the fix, updated the thread, and confirmed verification complete.\n\n19. The user asked \"what is next to do?\" again.\n\n20. The assistant re-read the next-steps prompts: agpack-prompt-next.md, agpack-prompt-step3.md, metered-web-broker-next-steps-prompt.md.\n\nNow let me identify the key facts and constraints.\n\n**Project context:** agpack is a portable, verifiable agent runtime at `/home/iconbaypark2900/dataScience/agpack`. It has a sandbox and a trust layer. The trust layer is \"Step 3.\"\n\n**Trust layer modules (Step 3):**\n- `trust/audit.py` \u2014 append-only replayable execution ledger (accountability half)\n- `trust/signing.py` \u2014 Ed25519 signature guarantee (cryptographic half)\n- `trust/delegation.py` \u2014 per-hop scoped tokens (authority half)\n\n**Test counts:**\n- test_trust_audit.py = 29\n- test_trust_delegation.py = 29\n- test_trust_signing.py = 24\n- test_sandbox_limits.py = 30\n- test_sandbox_host.py = 25\n- test_sandbox_imports.py = 28\n- test_sandbox_capabilities.py = 25\n- test_artifact_bundle.py = 13\n- Total = 203\n\n**Sandbox modules (Step 2, \"complete\"):**\n- capabilities.py \u2014 POLICY (what)\n- limits.py \u2014 RESOURCES (how much)\n- imports.py \u2014 surface\n- host.py \u2014 engine lifecycle\n\n**Key decision/reconciliation:** The stale `.scratch/reproduce_full.txt` showed \"10 failed / 72 passed\" but that was stale. The actual count is 203, all green. The assistant verified by reading, not running.\n\n**Production change:** validator.py already had `import pydantic` and `import yaml`. The real gap was that pyproject.toml''s `[project.dependencies]` only declared `cryptography>=42` \u2014 pydantic, PyYAML were undeclared runtime deps.\n\n**The fix that was applied:** The [user] un-commented/declared the 3 deps:\n```toml\ndependencies = [\n    \"pydantic>=2\",       # bundle schema + policy model validation (validator, schema)\n    \"cryptography>=42\",  # Ed25519 signing of bundle manifests (trust layer)\n    \"PyYAML>=6\",         # policy-component verification (validator._check_within_max_cap)\n]\n```\n\nAnd PyYAML>=6 was declared. wasmtime/opentelemetry-api/typer stayed commented.\n\n**Clean run:** python -m pytest -q from repo root: 203 passed in 0.43s.\n\n**Standing constraints:** \"never send without my approval\" \u2014 committing requires user approval. Actually let me re-check. The user said \"I won''t commit anything until you approve \u2014 that''s your standing rule.\" So the standing rule is: don''t commit/send without user approval.\n\nWait, actually let me look. The compaction note at the top says: \"including standing constraints stated at any point (e.g. ''never send without my approval'').\" So the example given is \"never send without my approval.\" Let me check if this was actually stated by the user. In the text: \"I won''t commit anything until you approve \u2014 that''s your standing rule.\" This is the assistant quoting the standing rule. The user didn''t explicitly restate it in this conversation, but it was referenced. And the previous compaction summary mentioned it. Let me preserve it.\n\nActually, I should be careful. The example in the prompt \"never send without my approval\" is just an example format. But it seems relevant here \u2014 the standing rule is \"don''t commit without approval.\" Let me note it.\n\nAlso: agpack source is READ-ONLY. The assistant \"cannot run pytest (read-only, no shell)\" \u2014 but wait, actually the user said they ran pytest. Let me clarify. The assistant cannot execute pytest itself, but the user (or environment) can run it. Actually the user said \"Done. Both parts verified.\" and mentioned \"python -m pytest -q from the repo root: 203 passed\". So the user ran it. Or was it done by the assistant? Let me re-read.\n\nThe [user] message says \"Done. Both parts verified.\" with the manifest fix and clean run. This suggests the user did it, OR the user is reporting. Given the assistant \"cannot run pytest,\" it seems the user has shell access and can run things. Actually, the workspace is at `/home/iconbaypark2900/openworker-tasks/1472646f-019` which is writable, but agpack itself at `/home/iconbaypark2900/dataScience/agpack` is read-only.\n\nHmm, but the user said the manifest was fixed \u2014 but agpack is read-only. So either the user has write access via some other means, or the \"read-only\" is from the assistant''s tool perspective. Let me not overstate. The key point: the assistant''s tools couldn''t write to agpack (read-only shell for the assistant), but the user made the change and ran pytest.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"[assistant] The user ''Done. Both parts verified.''\" \u2014 the manifest fix and the clean run were reported by the user. So the user did these actions (or had them done).\n\nLet me also note: the assistant could only read agpack, not execute commands there. The grep \"path escapes the workspace\" errors confirm the assistant''s grep/list tools are confined to the openworker-tasks workspace.\n\n**Files/artifacts:**\n\n1. `/home/iconbaypark2900/dataScience/agpack/pyproject.toml` \u2014 production manifest. Changed to