ody. Let me recall the state. In the message before:\n\n```\n[assistant \u2192 replace_in_file] {\"new\":\"def _nonneg_int(value: int, field: str) -> int:\\n \\\"\\\"\\\"Coerce ``value`` to a non-negative ``int`` or raise ``ValueError``.\\n\\n Rejects ``bool`` (a ``int`` subclass) and floats, because a ``\u2026\n```\n\nSo the parameter is now `value: int`. But the body still has the `isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int)` guard. With `value: int` type, ruff sees... hmm, actually the guard `not isinstance(value, int)` when value is typed int is dead code, but ruff''s TRY004 fires because inside a branch that''s entered only when the value is NOT the expected type. Actually TRY004 fires because the raise is inside `if ... not isinstance(value, int):` \u2014 ruff flags that raising ValueError for a type problem should be TypeError.\n\nWait, but actually the current error is at line 111. Let me look. The ruff output says:\n```\nTRY004 Prefer `TypeError` exception for invalid type\n --> src/agpack/sandbox/limits.py:111:9\n110 | if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int):\n111 | / raise ValueError(\n112 | | f\"{field} must be a\u2026\n```\n\nSo line 110 is the guard, line 111 is the raise. This is the `not isinstance(value, int)` branch. Because we''ve typed `value: int`, ruff knows that inside `not isinstance(value, int)`, the value can only be a non-int (since the declared type is int, this branch is technically unreachable, but the isinstance guard still narrows to object). \n\nActually the issue: the function is typed `value: int`, so at type-checking time ruff knows `value` is an int. But `not isinstance(value, int)` \u2014 ruff''s type inference: the guard is `isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int)`. If value is int, then `not isinstance(value, int)` is always False, and `isinstance(value, bool)` narrows value to bool (int subclass). So the whole condition is only true if value is a bool. But we raise ValueError there for