he content shown in the second tool result still shows ansi.js import. This is contradictory.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I''ll note in the summary that run.ts was being refactored to inline ANSI codes and drop the ansi.js shim dependency, and that there may be a leftover ansi.js file to reconcile. Actually, since I need to be concrete and not create confusion, I''ll state: \"run.ts refactored to inline ANSI escape codes; the ansi.js shim (created earlier) is being dropped as unused \u2014 verify no other file imports it.\"\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The safest is to note run.ts currently imports from ''./ansi.js'' (CONFORMANCE_OK, CONFORMANCE_WARN, reset), and the assistant intended to inline those codes and drop ansi.js. There''s a potential dangling ansi.js file to reconcile.\n\nLet me focus on what''s clearly established and load-bearing.\n\nNow, the key thing: The last action was writing validators.ts to fix contract-shape and settlement validators. So current work is finishing validators.ts.\n\nLet me note the terminal classes issue:\n- `FailureClass = ''blocked'' | ''payment-required'' | ''token-rejected''`\n- `TerminalKind` \u2014 the contract-shape validator gates on `fulfilled` because denied outcomes lack content/license/settlement.\n\nLet me think about the four terminal kinds. TerminalKind is likely ''fulfilled'' | ''denied'' | ''blocked'' | ... Let me check. From broker.ts, `TerminalKind` type. The dashboard/ledger renders terminal classes. The 4 terminal classes the broker reports \u2014 these are probably: fulfilled, denied, blocked, payment-required... Actually the validators mention \"4 terminal classes.\"\n\nFrom types.ts FailureClass: ''blocked'' | ''payment-required'' | ''token-rejected''. That''s 3. But the settlement validator checks \"4 terminal classes.\" Hmm. TerminalKind might be different. Let me note the settlement validator checks spend line sums across 4 terminal classes. I''ll just describe it.\n\nLet me not over-specify TerminalKind