 118-131 shows `makeSettlementKey`\n\nWait, that''s odd. Two read_file calls with same path/line range but different content? Let me look again.\n\n```\n[assistant \u2192 read_file] {\"path\": \"packages/rails/src/selfsettle.ts\", \"start_line\": 118, \"max_lines\": 14}\n...\n[tool result] {\"path\": \"packages/rails/src/selfsettle.ts\", \"start_line\": 118, ... content: ... signBytes/verifyBytes ...}\n\n[assistant \u2192 read_file] {\"path\": \"packages/rails/src/selfsettle.ts\", \"start_line\": 118, \"max_lines\": 14}\n...\n[tool result] {\"path\": \"packages/rails/src/selfsettle.ts\", \"start_line\": 118, ... content: ... makeSettlementKey ...}\n```\n\nHmm, these are contradictory. This might be a data inconsistency in the log, or the file changed between reads. Actually, looking more carefully, the second one might be a different read. Actually this could be that the first read_file result is stale/wrong. Let me not over-index on this discrepancy but note that selfsettle.ts was being read to locate bugs. Actually, I should note both possibilities but the most likely is: the assistant is reading selfsettle.ts to find the exact lines to fix. The makeSettlementKey one is probably the real content around 118-131. The signBytes one might be a stale result. I''ll just note that selfsettle.ts was read for bug investigation.\n\nActually, I''ll be careful. This discrepancy is minor and I shouldn''t fabricate. I''ll note selfsettle.ts was read multiple times to locate the buggy lines.\n\nNow let me also note the specific known errors in selfsettle.ts:\n1. Runtime: `this.server.key` (or `this.settlementKey`) read before constructor parameter property assignment \u2014 TS2729 \"Property ''server'' used before its initialization\"\n2. Type errors (8): likely RailError constructor shape, fromB64url, etc.\n3. Runtime test: AssertionError in one test, and 5 failures total\n\nLet me recall what core exports:\n- RailError from ''./errors.js''\n- fromB64url, b64url from util.ts\n\nThe fix direction: The selfsettle.ts is broken and needs fixing before the rail wiring can actually work.\n\nLet me now think about what the immediate next step is. The assistant was in the middle of reading selfsettle.ts to locate the exact buggy lines (makeSettlementKey at line ~118) so it could fix the constructor ordering bug and type errors. The very last tool result shows `makeSettlementKey` context, suggesting the assistant just confirmed where makeSettlementKey is.\n\nSo the immediate next step: fix selfsettle.ts \u2014 reorder constructor property assignments so `this.server` (or key) is assigned before being read, fix type errors (RailError, fromB64url usage), then re-run typecheck and vitest, then update the todo and report back to the user about the read-only question.\n\nLet me write the summary now.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole picture of what \"wire a real rail\" means and the dependencies:\n- `server.ts` buildRail() now imports makeSelf402Rail, makeSettlementKey, SettlementServer from @mwb/rails\n- Those depend on selfsettle.ts which is broken\n- So the wiring is done but non-functional until selfsettle.ts is fixed\n\nLet me also capture the env knobs documented:\n- RAIL_PRICE_MICROS (default 5000, \u2264 10000 so fetch reaches fulfilled)\n- SETTLEMENT_KEY_ID (key id stamped on signature)\n- (removed) RAIL_ID \u2014 SelfHosted402Rail hardcodes its id\n\nLet me verify: the edit removed RAIL_ID from env knobs. The replacement was:\n```\nconst priceMicros = Number(process.env.RIL_PRICE_MICROS ?? 5000);\n```\nWait, it says \"RIL_PRICE_MICROS\" \u2014 that might be another typo the assistant introduced! Let me check. In the replace_in_file:\n```\n\"new\": \" const priceMicros = Number(process.env.RIL_PRICE_MICROS ?? 5000);\"\n```\nHmm, \"RIL\" vs \"RAIL\". But then in the sed output it shows:\n```\nconst priceMicros = Number(process.env.RAIL_PRICE_MICROS ?? 5000);$\n```\nSo the actual file has \"RAIL_PRICE_MICROS\" \u2014 meaning the RIL one failed to match (found 0) and the real file has RAIL_PRICE_MICROS. Good, no typo. The assistant kept RIL_PRICE_MICROS in its attempts which all failed, and the file retained RAIL_PRICE_MICROS. Good.\n\nSo env knobs in final server.ts:\n- RAIL_PRICE_MICROS (default 5000)\n- SETTLEMENT_KEY_ID\n\nNow let me also capture the broker consumption:\n- broker only reads `rail.id` for error messages\n- No InMemoryRail-specific behavior assumed\n\nThe route handler in server.ts:\n- POST /fetch (POST /fetch with body { url })\n- broker.fetch({ url, ... })\n\nLet me capture the CI workflow: uses setup-node node-version 22, on push to main / PR.\n\nLet me write the summary.\n\nNow for the discrepancy in selfsettle.ts reads \u2014 I''ll just say it was read at those line ranges during bug investigation. I won''t fabricate content. Actually I should note the two conflicting reads happened; but for a summary it''s fine to just note the file was inspected at lines ~118-131 and ~384-415.\n\nLet me write it all out.\n\nFor \"All user messages\" \u2014 there''s only really the initial pasted status report (which was actually from a prior agent, shown as user turn). Then all subsequent \"user\" turns are tool results. Wait \u2014 the structure shows [tool result] markers which are tool outputs, and the only actual user message is the initial one. Let me re-read.\n\nThe structure:\n- [user] <status report ending with question>\n- [assistant \u2192 brain_recall]\n- [assistant \u2192 list_files]\n- [assistant] \"I''ll first check my memory...\"\n- [tool result] ...\n- [assistant] ...\n\nSo actually the only [user] message is the status report. Everything else is assistant tool calls and tool results. In the summary''s \"All user messages\" section, I should list that one initial message, plus note that the rest were tool-result-driven actions. Hmm, but the instruction says \"a chronological list of every user message.\" The tool results appear under [tool result] which are not user messages